build it where you can run it. the unit is what you can check out alone,
start and exercise — not what the host lets you edit in place.

the test: run it with the host gone. if it needs the host to boot, you
wrote a patch wearing a module's name.

what makes it liftable is what it does not reach for — no import upward,
no global the host set, no clock or socket it opened itself. hand those
in. `programming/commandquery:/call it twice/` is the same discipline one
call down.

it buys the loop, `general/loop:/edit to seen/`. a unit that starts in a
second gets edited a hundred times a day; one that needs the host's boot
gets edited when you must.

costs a seam, and a seam is a moving part. against
`general/simple:/one mechanism/`: pay it where you expect change, the
same place `programming/encapsulation` puts its barrier. one host, one
call site, no second reader — integrate and stop. pluggable on the
second, like every other generalisation here.

standalone is the property. `programming/simulator` is how you hold it
before the host exists.
